# Simple zeros of ̄ j on level-N curve count ν₃(N)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.natCard_ord_jBar_eq_one_eq_nuThree`
(`aa4b0a7b-4024-51de-9e2d-607e2b149481`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `ab083c4f-caed-5aca-acfb-344f7e88c85c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number. The ambient field is $\overline{F}_N :=$
`modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the intermediate field of the Laurent series field over
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise images of the
elements of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` (itself the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over
$\mathbb{Q}$ by the divisor expansions at level $N$), and $\bar j :=$ `jBar N` is the element of
$\overline{F}_N$ given by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion of $j$. Places of
$\overline{F}_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are valuation subrings of $\overline{F}_N$ containing
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, proper, and principal ideal rings, and $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the
logarithm of the associated adic valuation. Two hypotheses are assumed: first, at every place $v$
with $\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar j) > 0$ the integer $\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar j)$ divides $3$; second, the
number of places $v$ with $\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar j) > 0$ equals the number of elements $x$ of
`ModuliPoint N (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` with $j(x) = 0$, where `ModuliPoint N L` is the quotient of the
type of pairs (an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $L$, a point of additive order exactly $N$) by the
relation generated by a variable change together with replacing the marked point by a multiple by an
integer coprime to $N$. The conclusion is that the number of places $v$ of $\overline{F}_N$ with
$\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar j) = 1$ equals `nuThree N`, the number of $x \in \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$ with
$x^2 + x + 1 = 0$.

This is the count of the elliptic points of order three on the level-$N$ modular curve, realised
here as the number of simple zeros of $\bar j$ on the geometric function field $\overline{F}_N$; the
two hypotheses isolate the local input (only orders $1$ and $3$ occur above $j = 0$) and the mass
formula comparison with the moduli count. It is used in the construction of divisors with prescribed
degree and integrality from Riemann–Roch data and in the derivation of the genus formula for
$\overline{F}_N$.

